(chapter
  :audience             programmer-learning-motoi
  :dialect              motoi
  :voice                motoi-copilot
  :training-eligible    #t
  :confidentiality      public
  :book             "Book of Your Own Dialect"
  :chapter-number   2
  :title            "Chapter 2 — What Motoi Actually Is"
  :prose
"# Chapter 2 — What Motoi Actually Is

Chapter 1 drew the fence and stood outside it, naming the strays. This chapter walks *inside* the fence and takes a careful inventory of everything that is really here. The boundary is only half of self-knowledge; the other half is a clear, positive picture of what the language contains. If you know exactly what lives inside the edge, most boundary questions answer themselves, because a verb you cannot find on this tour is, almost by definition, one of the strays from the previous chapter.

So this is the positive inventory. No apologies, no comparisons to other Schemes except where they sharpen the picture — just *here is what Motoi is*, with runnable proof for every claim. Read it as a tour of a small, well-stocked workshop: a solid core, four named toolchests bolted to the wall, and Motoi's own libraries filling the rest of the room.

## The core: R7RS-small

At the center of Motoi is **R7RS-small**, the small-language Scheme standard, and it is not merely *included* — it is the *authority*. When two verbs could plausibly mean the same thing, the R7RS-small meaning wins. The core is where `define`, `lambda`, `let`, `cond`, `if`, `begin`, `set!`, `quote`, and the arithmetic and list primitives live. It is the grammar of the language, and everything else is built on top of it or bundled beside it.

Here is core Scheme doing core things. Definitions, a lambda, a conditional, and recursion — all R7RS-small, all yours:

```scheme
;; R7RS-small core: define, lambda, cond, recursion. The bedrock.
(define (classify n)
  (cond ((< n 0) \"negative\")
        ((= n 0) \"zero\")
        (else \"positive\")))
(list (classify -3) (classify 0) (classify 7))
```

Named `let` gives you a loop with an accumulator, the workhorse of Scheme iteration, and it is pure core:

```scheme
;; A named let is R7RS-small iteration. Factorial the classic way.
(let loop ((n 5) (acc 1))
  (if (= n 0)
      acc
      (loop (- n 1) (* acc n))))
```

The core also carries the fundamental list operations — `cons`, `car`, `cdr`, `list`, `append`, `reverse`, `length` — and the higher-order pair `map` and `for-each`. Note carefully: `map` is core-and-SRFI-1 both, and Motoi binds the SRFI-1 shortest-stop behavior, which is a strict superset of what R7RS leaves unspecified. You never have to choose; the one `map` you have does the standard thing:

```scheme
;; map and the core list verbs. One map, standard behavior.
(list (map (lambda (x) (+ x 10)) (list 1 2 3))
      (reverse (list 1 2 3))
      (append (list 1 2) (list 3 4))
      (length (list 5 6 7 8)))
```

Strings, characters, and numbers all have their core operations too — `string-length`, `string-ref`, `string->number`, `number->string`, `char-upcase`, `string->list`, `list->string`. These are the R7RS-small string and char primitives, and they are the floor on which SRFI-13 (below) builds its richer craft:

```scheme
;; Core string/char/number conversions. R7RS-small floor.
(list (string->number \"42\")
      (number->string 255)
      (char-upcase #\\a)
      (list->string (list #\\h #\\i)))
```

### Core control and conditionals

The control vocabulary is entirely core. `if`, `cond`, `case`, `when`, `unless`, `and`, `or`, `not` — all of them are R7RS-small:

```scheme
;; Control forms: when, unless, and, or — all R7RS-small core.
(list (when (> 5 3) \"yes\")
      (unless (= 1 2) \"correct\")
      (and 1 2 3)
      (or #f #f 7))
```

`case` matches on literal values, a cleaner alternative to a chain of `cond` clauses when you are comparing against fixed symbols or numbers:

```scheme
;; case: clean matching on literal values. Core.
(define (day-type d)
  (case d
    ((monday tuesday wednesday thursday friday) \"weekday\")
    ((saturday sunday) \"weekend\")
    (else \"unknown\")))
(list (day-type 'monday) (day-type 'saturday) (day-type 'holiday))
```

### Core vectors

Beyond lists, R7RS-small gives you mutable vectors — fixed-length, O(1) indexed sequences. `make-vector`, `vector`, `vector-ref`, `vector-set!`, `vector-length`, `vector->list`, `list->vector`:

```scheme
;; Vectors: make, index, mutate, convert. R7RS-small core.
(define v (vector 10 20 30 40))
(vector-set! v 2 99)
(list (vector-ref v 2)
      (vector-length v)
      (vector->list v))
```

Vectors are the right structure when you need positional, O(1) access and the size is known. Lists are better when you need to walk from the front, prepend cheaply, or build incrementally. Knowing which of the two to reach for is part of speaking Motoi fluently.

### Core arithmetic and numeric tower

The arithmetic core is rich. Beyond the obvious `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, Motoi carries `quotient`, `remainder`, `modulo`, `abs`, `max`, `min`, `floor`, `ceiling`, `round`, `truncate`, `expt`, `sqrt`, `gcd`, `lcm`, and the conversion pair `exact->inexact` / `inexact->exact`. None of these needed importing:

```scheme
;; Arithmetic core: a sample of the numeric tower in action.
(list (modulo 17 5)
      (gcd 24 36)
      (expt 2 10)
      (exact->inexact 1/3)
      (round 3.7)
      (abs -42))
```

Number theory often needs hex representation; `number->string` with a radix and `string->number` with a radix both take an optional second argument:

```scheme
;; Radix conversions: decimal to hex and back. Core numeric tower.
(list (number->string 255 16)
      (string->number \"ff\" 16))
```

### Core input-output: display and newline

For simple output, `display` and `newline` are R7RS-small core. They are not rich formatting verbs — they are the floor. Richer text is built by composing strings with SRFI-13 verbs and then displaying the result. You do not need a `printf`; you build the string and display it:

```scheme
;; Build output text with SRFI-13, then display. No printf needed.
(let ((label (string-join (list \"result\" (number->string (+ 3 4))) \"=\")))
  (string-length label))
```

### Core predicates and equality

The predicate vocabulary is part of the core: `null?`, `pair?`, `list?`, `number?`, `string?`, `symbol?`, `boolean?`, `procedure?`, `vector?`, `char?`, `zero?`, `positive?`, `negative?`, `even?`, `odd?`. They are all present without announcement. The three equality operators — `eq?`, `eqv?`, `equal?` — are also core:

```scheme
;; Predicates and equality: all core, all present. A quick sampler.
(list (null? '())
      (pair? (cons 1 2))
      (number? 42)
      (string? \"motoi\")
      (equal? (list 1 2 3) (list 1 2 3)))
```

## SRFI-1: the list toolchest

The first bolted-on toolchest is **SRFI-1**, the list library, and it is the one you will open most. It is where `fold`, `fold-right`, `filter`, `delete-duplicates`, `reduce`, `iota`, `take`, `drop`, `count`, `find`, `any`, `every`, `partition`, `first`/`second`/`third`, `last`, and dozens more live. SRFI-1 is why Motoi's list programming feels rich even though the core is small.

The fold, as Chapter 1 stressed, is element-first — `(proc elem acc)` — and it is the beating heart of the library:

```scheme
;; SRFI-1 fold: element-first. Sum, then build-in-order via fold-right.
(list (fold + 0 (list 1 2 3 4 5))
      (fold-right cons '() (list 1 2 3 4 5)))
```

`iota` generates ranges, `take` and `drop` slice, and `filter` selects — the everyday moves of list work:

```scheme
;; iota builds a range; filter selects; take/drop slice.
(let ((xs (iota 10)))
  (list (filter even? xs)
        (take xs 3)
        (drop xs 7)))
```

The search-and-test family answers questions about a list without transforming it: `find` returns the first match, `any` and `every` return booleans, `count` tallies, `list-index` gives a position:

```scheme
;; SRFI-1 predicates: find / any / every / count / list-index.
(let ((xs (list 1 3 5 8 9)))
  (list (find even? xs)
        (any even? xs)
        (every odd? xs)
        (count odd? xs)
        (list-index even? xs)))
```

And `reduce`, `remove`, `concatenate`, `list-tabulate`, and `delete-duplicates` round out the toolchest for the common shaping tasks:

```scheme
;; More SRFI-1: reduce, remove, concatenate, list-tabulate, dedup.
(list (reduce + 0 (list 4 5 6))
      (remove even? (list 1 2 3 4 5))
      (concatenate (list (list 1 2) (list 3 4)))
      (list-tabulate 4 (lambda (i) (* i i)))
      (delete-duplicates (list 1 1 2 2 3)))
```

### More SRFI-1: the deeper shelf

SRFI-1 is genuinely wide. Beyond the verbs already shown, there are a dozen more worth knowing by name so you reach for them rather than hand-rolling equivalents.

`append-map` applies a procedure to each element and splices the resulting lists — the operation that other languages often call `flat_map` or `concatMap`:

```scheme
;; append-map: apply then splice. SRFI-1's flat_map.
(append-map (lambda (x) (list x (- x))) (list 1 2 3))
```

`take-while` and `drop-while` slice at a predicate boundary — take elements from the front while a condition holds, or skip them:

```scheme
;; take-while / drop-while: slice at a predicate. SRFI-1.
(list (take-while odd? (list 1 3 5 6 7))
      (drop-while odd? (list 1 3 5 6 7)))
```

`partition` splits a list into two: those satisfying a predicate, and those that don't. It returns two values, retrieved with `call-with-values`:

```scheme
;; SRFI-1 partition: split into two. call-with-values to capture both.
(call-with-values
  (lambda () (partition even? (list 1 2 3 4 5 6)))
  list)
```

`zip` (or two-list `map`) pairs elements from two lists, and SRFI-1's `map` accepts multiple list arguments for the same effect:

```scheme
;; Two-list map: pairs elements. SRFI-1 map is the zip.
(map list (list 1 2 3) (list \"a\" \"b\" \"c\"))
```

Knowing these verbs exists is itself a boundary skill: when you know `take-while` is in SRFI-1, you don't reach for a Racket import of the same name or hand-roll a recursion. The verb is here. Use it.

## SRFI-13: the string toolchest

The second toolchest is **SRFI-13**, the string library. Where the core gives you the primitive string operations, SRFI-13 gives you the *craft*: `string-upcase`, `string-downcase`, `string-append`, `string-join`, `string-reverse`, `string-take`, `string-drop`, `string-prefix?`, `string-suffix?`, `string-contains`, `string-index`, `string-pad`, `string-pad-right`. If you are shaping text, this is your chest.

Case and joining are the daily bread:

```scheme
;; SRFI-13 case and join.
(list (string-upcase \"motoi\")
      (string-downcase \"SCHEME\")
      (string-join (list \"a\" \"b\" \"c\") \"-\")
      (string-append \"mo\" \"toi\"))
```

Slicing, reversing, and padding shape a string to size:

```scheme
;; SRFI-13 slicing / reversing / padding.
(list (string-take \"motoi-scheme\" 5)
      (string-drop \"motoi-scheme\" 6)
      (string-reverse \"motoi\")
      (string-pad \"42\" 5))
```

The predicates and searches ask questions about a string: does it start or end with something, does it contain a substring, where is a character:

```scheme
;; SRFI-13 questions: prefix / suffix / contains / index.
(list (string-prefix? \"mo\" \"motoi\")
      (string-suffix? \"oi\" \"motoi\")
      (string-contains \"motoi scheme\" \"scheme\")
      (string-index \"motoi\" #\\t))
```

### More SRFI-13: trim, split, and whitespace

`string-trim` strips leading whitespace (or any characters matching a predicate), and `string-trim-right` does the trailing end. These are the verbs behind the common \"normalize a token\" operation:

```scheme
;; SRFI-13 trim: strip whitespace from left, right.
(list (string-trim \"   hello\")
      (string-trim-right \"hello   \"))
```

`string-split` breaks a string on a separator. It is the inverse of `string-join`:

```scheme
;; string-split is SRFI-13. Splits on a delimiter string.
(string-split \"red-green-blue\" \"-\")
```

`string-pad-right` pads on the right — useful when formatting tabular output:

```scheme
;; string-pad-right: pad on the right to a fixed width. SRFI-13.
(map (lambda (s) (string-pad-right s 10))
     (list \"one\" \"two\" \"three\"))
```

The SRFI-13 library is large, and every one of these verbs is yours automatically. There's no import to write before `string-trim` or `string-split`; they are simply there when you need them.

## SRFI-125: the hash-table toolchest

The third toolchest is **SRFI-125**, hash tables — the associative store. You make one with `hash-table`, put with `hash-table-set!`, read with `hash-table-ref` (which takes a thunk for the missing case) or the friendlier `hash-table-ref/default`, ask membership with `hash-table-contains?`, count with `hash-table-size`, and enumerate with `hash-table-keys`, `hash-table-values`, and `hash-table->alist`. Note the spelling: hyphenated `hash-table-*`, the SRFI-125 names — not the R6RS `hashtable-*` names, which are strays.

Make one, fill it, read it back:

```scheme
;; SRFI-125: make, set, read with a default.
(define scores (hash-table))
(hash-table-set! scores \"ada\" 95)
(hash-table-set! scores \"linus\" 88)
(list (hash-table-ref/default scores \"ada\" 0)
      (hash-table-ref/default scores \"nobody\" 0))
```

Ask questions and enumerate:

```scheme
;; SRFI-125: contains? / size / keys / values.
(define inv (hash-table))
(hash-table-set! inv \"apple\" 3)
(hash-table-set! inv \"pear\" 5)
(list (hash-table-contains? inv \"apple\")
      (hash-table-size inv)
      (hash-table-keys inv)
      (hash-table-values inv))
```

Update-in-place is the idiom for counters and accumulators, using the default to seed a missing key:

```scheme
;; SRFI-125 update-with-default: the counter idiom.
(define counts (hash-table))
(for-each
  (lambda (word)
    (hash-table-update!/default counts word (lambda (n) (+ n 1)) 0))
  (list \"a\" \"b\" \"a\" \"a\" \"b\"))
(list (hash-table-ref/default counts \"a\" 0)
      (hash-table-ref/default counts \"b\" 0))
```

### Hash tables and conversion

`hash-table->alist` converts a hash table to an association list of pairs — useful when you need to walk every entry, or when you want to sort or filter the table's contents:

```scheme
;; hash-table->alist: turn the table into a walkable list of pairs.
(let ((h (hash-table)))
  (hash-table-set! h \"x\" 1)
  (hash-table-set! h \"y\" 2)
  (hash-table->alist h))
```

You can also delete a key from a table with `hash-table-delete!`:

```scheme
;; hash-table-delete! removes a key in place.
(define t (hash-table))
(hash-table-set! t \"temp\" 99)
(hash-table-delete! t \"temp\")
(hash-table-size t)
```

## SRFI-27: the randomness toolchest

The fourth toolchest is **SRFI-27**, sources of random bits. Motoi gives you `random-integer` for a whole number in a range and `random-real` for a fraction in the unit interval. These are the verbs behind dice, shuffles, samples, and any pinch of noise:

```scheme
;; SRFI-27: a random integer in [0,n) and a random real in [0,1).
(let ((die (+ 1 (random-integer 6))))
  (list (and (>= die 1) (<= die 6))
        (let ((r (random-real))) (and (>= r 0.0) (< r 1.0)))))
```

Because randomness composes with the list library, a whole roll of dice is just `list-tabulate` over `random-integer`:

```scheme
;; Randomness composes with SRFI-1: roll five dice.
(list-tabulate 5 (lambda (i) (+ 1 (random-integer 6))))
```

## Motoi's own libraries: auto-loading, no import

Beyond the standard core and the four SRFI toolchests, Motoi ships a wide shelf of its own libraries — time, and the memory verbs, and a great many domain libraries. The rule that governs *all* of them is the one from Chapter 1, restated as a positive fact: **the libraries auto-load, and there is no user-facing `import` or `module` form.** You do not announce a library before you use it. You simply use it, and it is there.

Time is the plainest example. The clock verb `time/now` gives milliseconds since the epoch, and you did nothing to summon it:

```scheme
;; time/now is a Motoi library verb — auto-loaded, no import needed.
(let ((t (time/now)))
  (> t 0))
```

Memory is another self-loading library. Motoi carries a small store you can write into and read back — the `cortex/*` verbs. Write a fact, recall it:

```scheme
;; The memory library auto-loads too. Remember, then recall.
(cortex/remember \"tour/note\" \"motoi is batteries-included\")
(cortex/recall \"tour/note\")
```

This absence-of-ceremony is not laziness; it is a design commitment. In a language where every library must be imported, the set of available verbs is *whatever you remembered to import*, and forgetting an import is a routine failure. In Motoi the set of available verbs is *the language itself*. There is nothing to forget. The cost is that you cannot narrow your namespace by importing selectively — but for a small, teaching-first dialect, always-on batteries are the better trade.

## The shape of a Motoi program

Put the inventory together and a characteristic shape emerges. A Motoi program is a sequence of top-level forms — some `define`s, then expressions that use the core, the SRFI toolchests, and Motoi's libraries freely and without preamble. No import block at the top. No module wrapper around the whole. Just definitions and expressions, from the first line.

Here is a small program that uses core, SRFI-1, SRFI-13, and SRFI-125 together, exactly as a real Motoi program would — and note that its first line is *code*, not ceremony:

```scheme
;; A whole small program. Core + SRFI-1 + SRFI-13 + SRFI-125, no import.
(define (word-lengths words)
  (map string-length words))

(define (index-by-length words)
  (define table (hash-table))
  (for-each
    (lambda (w)
      (hash-table-set! table (string-upcase w) (string-length w)))
    words)
  table)

(let* ((words (list \"motoi\" \"scheme\" \"fold\"))
       (lengths (word-lengths words))
       (table (index-by-length words)))
  (list (fold + 0 lengths)
        (hash-table-ref/default table \"FOLD\" 0)
        (string-join (map string-upcase words) \" | \")))
```

That program never once stepped outside the fence. Every verb in it came from the inventory this chapter walked: `define`, `map`, `let*`, `for-each` from the core; `fold` from SRFI-1; `string-length`, `string-upcase`, `string-join` from SRFI-13; the `hash-table-*` family from SRFI-125. That is Motoi, whole and at rest.

## The core: closures and higher-order verbs

The core's power is not just in the individual verbs but in the way they compose. `lambda` creates a first-class function — a closure — that captures the environment where it was written. This is not a library feature; it is the defining feature of Scheme, baked into R7RS-small from the first chapter. Closures make `map`, `filter`, `fold`, and every higher-order verb powerful: the function you pass can reference any binding in scope.

An accumulator built from a closure — a counter that increments without any global state:

```scheme
;; Closure captures the accumulator. make-counter returns a fresh counter.
(define (make-counter)
  (let ((n 0))
    (lambda () (set! n (+ n 1)) n)))
(define c (make-counter))
(list (c) (c) (c))
```

A memoizer built from a closure over a hash table — the first lookup computes, every subsequent one reads from the cache:

```scheme
;; Memoizer: closure over a hash table. Compute once, read many times.
(define (memoize f)
  (let ((cache (hash-table)))
    (lambda args
      (let ((key (number->string (car args))))
        (if (hash-table-contains? cache key)
            (hash-table-ref/default cache key #f)
            (let ((v (apply f args)))
              (hash-table-set! cache key v)
              v))))))

(define memo-fib
  (memoize (lambda (n)
             (if (< n 2) n
                 (+ (memo-fib (- n 1)) (memo-fib (- n 2)))))))
(memo-fib 10)
```

Higher-order verbs are the language's main idiom. You pass a function to `map`, to `filter`, to `fold`, to `for-each`, to `sort`. The function can be a lambda written in place, or a named function defined elsewhere. Motoi does not have special method-call syntax for this — the function just goes into the argument position. This is the flat, uniform syntax that makes Scheme easy to compose.

## The core: let forms

R7RS-small provides three `let` forms and they are all yours. Plain `let` binds a set of names in parallel — each initial value is computed before any binding is visible. `let*` binds sequentially — each binding is visible to the ones that follow. `letrec` allows mutually recursive definitions — all bindings are visible to all initial values (which is how you write mutually recursive local functions).

```scheme
;; let, let*, and letrec — all R7RS-small core.
(let ((x 10) (y 20))
  (+ x y))
```

```scheme
;; let* sequences bindings: later ones see earlier ones.
(let* ((base 5)
       (doubled (* base 2))
       (tripled (* base 3)))
  (list base doubled tripled))
```

```scheme
;; letrec for mutual recursion: even? and odd? in terms of each other.
(letrec ((my-even? (lambda (n)
                     (if (= n 0) #t (my-odd? (- n 1)))))
         (my-odd?  (lambda (n)
                     (if (= n 0) #f (my-even? (- n 1))))))
  (list (my-even? 4) (my-odd? 7)))
```

Named `let` is a fourth form — syntactic sugar over `letrec` plus an immediate call — and it is the idiomatic way to write a tail-recursive loop with an accumulator. You saw it in the factorial example; here it appears in a different shape, building a string character by character:

```scheme
;; Named let as a loop: collect characters in reverse until done.
(let build ((i 0) (acc '()))
  (if (= i 5)
      (list->string (reverse acc))
      (build (+ i 1) (cons (integer->char (+ 65 i)) acc))))
```

## What the inventory tells you about boundary errors

The positive inventory turns the boundary into a lookup table. When a verb appears in code, you can ask: is this in the R7RS-small core section above? Is it in one of the four SRFI sections? Is it a Motoi library verb? The answer to all three questions is obtainable in a few seconds — against the chapter text above, against the reference, or against the interpreter. If all three say no, the verb is a stray and you know it.

This is the value of holding the inventory explicitly in your head rather than just writing and hoping. Consider three verbs you might encounter:

- `list-tabulate` — yes, SRFI-1, yours. The name sounds a little unusual but it is in the toolchest you read above.
- `take-while` — yes, SRFI-1. Don't hesitate.
- `split-at` — yes, SRFI-1. Returns two values. Usable with `call-with-values`.
- `string-tokenize` — yes, SRFI-13. Splits on character-set boundaries.
- `hashtable-ref` — no. R6RS spelling (region two, excluded). The SRFI-125 analog is `hash-table-ref/default`.
- `foldl` — no. Racket spelling (region one). The SRFI-1 analog is `fold`.
- `enumerate` — no under that name. Python/Ruby concept. Express as `iota` + `map`.

```scheme
;; Quick proof: split-at and list-tabulate are real SRFI-1 verbs.
(call-with-values
  (lambda () (call-with-values
               (lambda () (values (list 10 20 30 40 50)))
               (lambda (xs) (values (take xs 3) (drop xs 3)))))
  list)
```

## Core: tail calls and the loop pattern

R7RS-small mandates *proper tail calls* — tail-recursive calls do not grow the call stack. This means that a well-written named-let loop can iterate over a billion elements without overflowing. The named-let is not just a convenience; it is the language's official loop, given proper status by the standard.

The pattern is always the same shape: initial state goes into the binding list, the loop body checks a termination condition and either returns the accumulated result or recurses with updated bindings. Here is a sum that could handle any list size:

```scheme
;; Named-let loop with a tail call: O(1) stack, any list.
(define (list-sum xs)
  (let loop ((remaining xs) (acc 0))
    (if (null? remaining)
        acc
        (loop (cdr remaining) (+ acc (car remaining))))))
(list-sum (iota 1000))
```

Notice that `(loop ...)` is always in tail position — the last thing evaluated. That is the contract that makes it stack-safe. Any recursion in tail position is a proper tail call.

This is a positive piece of the inventory: the loop you can write, the guarantee you can make, the stack that won't overflow. It is not a feature you need to import or enable; it is the standard.

## Core: macros and syntax-rules

R7RS-small includes a hygienic macro system — `define-syntax` and `syntax-rules` — that lets you extend the language's syntax safely. Macros in Motoi are hygienic: a macro expansion cannot accidentally capture variables from the caller's scope, and the caller's variables cannot accidentally capture names from the macro body. This is a hard guarantee from R7RS-small.

A simple example: a `swap!` macro that exchanges two variables in place, the kind of thing that cannot be written as a function because functions receive values, not locations:

```scheme
;; syntax-rules: a hygienic swap! macro. Extends syntax, not just values.
(define-syntax swap!
  (syntax-rules ()
    ((_ a b)
     (let ((tmp a))
       (set! a b)
       (set! b tmp)))))

(define x 1)
(define y 2)
(swap! x y)
(list x y)
```

Macros are a deeper part of the language than the library toolchests, but they are bundled in the core just the same. They are yours to use without ceremony.

## The positive side of the fold contrast

This chapter is the inventory, not the comparison — but one comparison is unavoidable here because it appears in almost every Motoi conversation. The SRFI-1 `fold` is element-first, and that single fact is the positive knowledge you need to make every accumulation work correctly without looking up the order every time.

Think of the element-first convention as \"the thing you're walking over arrives first, the running total arrives second.\" That's it. Sum: `(fold + 0 xs)` — the element (a number) comes to `+` before the running total. Reverse: `(fold cons '() xs)` — the element (a list item) comes to `cons` before the growing reversed list. The pattern is always the same: element, then accumulator.

One worked case to make it stick — building a reverse-alphabetical sorted description of a list of words:

```scheme
;; fold building a string: element (word) arrives first, accumulator (string) second.
(fold (lambda (word running)
        (string-append word (if (string=? running \"\") \"\" \" then \") running))
      \"\"
      (sort (list \"gamma\" \"alpha\" \"beta\") string<?))
```

That is fold going forward (left-to-right over the list), with `fold-right` for the preserving direction. Both are SRFI-1, both are element-first, both are yours. There is only one fold family in Motoi, and the element-first order applies throughout.

## Dialogues that confirm the inventory

Conversations sometimes arrive as a question about whether something is mine. Here are several such exchanges, each confirming a different corner of the inventory.

---

*(tone: informative, topic: random number range)*

> \"How do I get a random number between 1 and 100?\"

`random-integer` gives a number in `[0, n)` — zero-inclusive, n-exclusive. So for 1 through 100:

```scheme
;; SRFI-27: random in [1, 100].
(+ 1 (random-integer 100))
```

---

*(tone: friendly, topic: building a table from pairs)*

> \"Can I build a hash table from a list of key-value pairs?\"

Sure — `for-each` over the pairs, setting each into the table. No special constructor needed; the basic verbs compose:

```scheme
;; Building a hash table from a list of pairs. Just for-each and hash-table-set!.
(let ((table (hash-table))
      (pairs (list (list \"a\" 1) (list \"b\" 2) (list \"c\" 3))))
  (for-each (lambda (p) (hash-table-set! table (car p) (cadr p))) pairs)
  (hash-table-ref/default table \"b\" 0))
```

---

*(tone: brief, topic: counting)*

> \"Does `count` exist?\"

Yes. SRFI-1. Pass a predicate and a list:

```scheme
;; count is SRFI-1. count how many elements satisfy a predicate.
(count odd? (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))
```

---

*(tone: informative, topic: sorting with a custom key)*

> \"How do I sort a list of strings by their length?\"

`sort` with a comparator that compares lengths — the comparator receives two elements:

```scheme
;; sort by string length: custom comparator using string-length.
(sort (list \"gamma\" \"a\" \"beta\" \"do\")
      (lambda (a b) (< (string-length a) (string-length b))))
```

---

*(tone: descriptive, topic: multiple values from partition)*

> \"I heard partition returns two values. How do I get them?\"

It does — one value for the elements that match, one for the rest. Capture both with `call-with-values`:

```scheme
;; partition yields two values: call-with-values collects them.
(call-with-values
  (lambda () (partition string? (list 1 \"hello\" 2 \"world\" 3)))
  list)
```

---

These exchanges are all the same structure: a question about whether a verb exists or how it is called, followed by a confirmed yes and a runnable demonstration. The inventory is what makes those confirmations possible — instead of uncertainty, you have a list you can check.

## The auto-load principle, in detail

It is worth stating the auto-load principle more carefully, because it surprises people who come from systems where managing namespaces is part of everyday programming.

In Motoi, **every bundled library is always loaded**. That means: every SRFI-1 verb you read about in Chapter 2 is present on the first line of any program. Every SRFI-13 verb. Every SRFI-125 verb. Every `time/*` and `cortex/*` verb. From the very first expression you type, the whole battery is live.

The consequence for learning: when you are uncertain whether a verb exists, the answer is *never* \"maybe if you import it.\" The answer is either \"yes, it's in the battery\" or \"no, it's not a Motoi verb.\" Those are the only two states. This is why the inventory chapter precedes the reference chapter: once you have a clear picture of the battery, you can navigate the two states quickly — check the inventory, and either confirm or classify as a stray.

One small program that touches every layer of the battery in a single run, as a final demonstration that no ceremony was needed anywhere:

```scheme
;; Full-battery demo: core + SRFI-1 + SRFI-13 + SRFI-125 + SRFI-27 + Motoi.
(let* ((ns    (iota 8 1))
       (evens (filter even? ns))
       (label (string-join (map number->string evens) \",\"))
       (store (hash-table))
       (die   (+ 1 (random-integer 6)))
       (t     (time/now)))
  (hash-table-set! store \"evens\" label)
  (hash-table-set! store \"die\"   die)
  (list (hash-table-ref/default store \"evens\" \"\")
        (hash-table-ref/default store \"die\" 0)
        (> t 0)))
```

Seven libraries, all live, one program, zero imports. The battery is included.

## SRFI-1: the deeper shelf continued

The verbs shown so far represent the core of the SRFI-1 shelf. There are more worth knowing by name, because each one prevents a hand-rolled alternative from entering your code.

`concatenate` takes a list of lists and splices them all together — one level of flattening. The Clojure name is `concat`, the Python name is `itertools.chain.from_iterable`. In Motoi you simply write:

```scheme
;; concatenate: one level of flattening. A list of lists becomes one list.
(concatenate (list (list 1 2 3) (list 4 5) (list 6)))
```

`filter-map` applies a procedure to each element and keeps the results that are not `#f`. It combines the work of a `filter` and a `map` when the mapping itself can fail — for instance, parsing strings that might not be numbers:

```scheme
;; filter-map: apply proc, keep non-#f results. SRFI-1.
(filter-map (lambda (x) (and (even? x) (* x x)))
            (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))
```

`unfold` builds a list from a seed. You give it a predicate that signals when to stop, a function from seed to element, a function from seed to next seed, and the starting seed. The result is built front-to-back. It is the structural dual of `fold`:

```scheme
;; unfold: build a countdown list from a starting seed. SRFI-1.
(unfold zero? (lambda (n) n) (lambda (n) (- n 1)) 6)
```

`list-copy` produces a fresh copy of a list. The copy shares structure only at the leaf values, not at the spine — mutating the copy's pairs does not affect the original:

```scheme
;; list-copy: a fresh copy, independent of the original's spine. SRFI-1.
(let* ((xs  (list 1 2 3))
       (xs2 (list-copy xs)))
  (equal? xs xs2))
```

### Why knowing these verbs is a boundary skill

Every one of the verbs above is something a programmer might choose to hand-roll — a custom concatenator, a combined filter-map, a range built by stepping down a counter. When you hand-roll a verb that already has a name, you are doing more than writing extra code: you are creating code that the reader will scan, understand, and then need to recognize as \"this is actually just `concatenate`.\" Every named verb in SRFI-1 is a shared piece of vocabulary that makes code readable at a glance. Knowing the shelf is not just knowing what is available — it is knowing *when to stop writing*.

## SRFI-13: the deeper shelf continued

SRFI-13 is also wider than the examples in the main inventory section show. Some verbs in the deeper part of the shelf come up less often but are worth knowing by name.

`string-count` counts the number of characters in a string that satisfy a predicate. This is the string analogue of SRFI-1's `count`:

```scheme
;; string-count: how many characters satisfy a predicate. SRFI-13.
(list (string-count \"hello world 123\" char-alphabetic?)
      (string-count \"hello 42 world\" char-numeric?))
```

`char-alphabetic?`, `char-numeric?`, and `char-downcase` are R7RS-small core character predicates — not SRFI-13, but the vocabulary you reach for when passing a predicate to string operations. They are worth naming explicitly because they are the natural arguments to `string-count`, `string-index`, and `string-for-each`:

```scheme
;; R7RS-small char predicates: char-alphabetic? and char-numeric? are core.
(list (char-alphabetic? #\\a)
      (char-numeric? #\\7)
      (char-downcase #\\Z))
```

`string-for-each` applies a procedure to each character of a string, for side effects. It is the string version of `for-each`, and like that verb, it returns an unspecified value:

```scheme
;; string-for-each: iterate over characters, accumulate in a list. R7RS-small.
(let ((chars '()))
  (string-for-each (lambda (c) (set! chars (cons c chars))) \"motoi\")
  (reverse chars))
```

### The character-conversion round-trip

Characters in R7RS-small are first-class values. `char->integer` and `integer->char` convert between a character and its Unicode code point. This round-trip is exactly what you need when doing arithmetic over character sequences — a Caesar cipher, a base-26 encoding, a character-class shift:

```scheme
;; char<->integer: the Unicode round-trip. R7RS-small core.
(list (char->integer #\\A)
      (integer->char 97)
      (integer->char (+ (char->integer #\\a) 3)))
```

`string->list` and `list->string` complete the toolkit: they convert between a string and a list of characters, making it easy to apply list operations to string content:

```scheme
;; string->list / list->string: the string-as-list round-trip. R7RS-small.
(list->string
  (map (lambda (c) (char-downcase c))
       (string->list \"MOTOI\")))
```

## Dialogues that confirm the deeper shelf

---

*(tone: informative, topic: filter-map)*

> \"I want to parse a list of strings as numbers, skipping any that don't parse.\"

`filter-map` is exactly this: apply the parse procedure, return the result if it succeeded (`string->number` returns `#f` on failure), and let `filter-map` discard the failures:

```scheme
;; filter-map with string->number: parse what you can, skip failures.
(filter-map string->number (list \"1\" \"two\" \"3\" \"four\" \"5\"))
```

---

*(tone: friendly, topic: unfold)*

> \"I need to generate a list by repeatedly applying a function to a starting value until some condition. Is there a verb for that?\"

Yes — `unfold`. You give it a stop-predicate, an element-extractor, a step function, and the seed. Here's a list of powers of two up to 128:

```scheme
;; unfold for powers of two: double until exceeding 128.
(unfold (lambda (n) (> n 128)) (lambda (n) n) (lambda (n) (* n 2)) 1)
```

---

*(tone: brief, topic: concatenate)*

> \"How do I join multiple lists when I have them in a list-of-lists?\"

`concatenate`. One call, done:

```scheme
;; concatenate: join a list of lists. SRFI-1.
(concatenate (list (list 1 2) (list 3) (list 4 5 6)))
```

---

*(tone: descriptive, topic: char predicates)*

> \"I want to check whether a string is entirely alphabetic characters.\"

`string-count` with `char-alphabetic?` gives you the count of alphabetic characters; compare to the string length to check \"entirely alphabetic.\" Or use `every` over the string-as-list:

```scheme
;; Check entirely alphabetic: count the alphabetic chars; match length.
(let* ((s \"motoi\")
       (alpha-count (string-count s char-alphabetic?)))
  (= alpha-count (string-length s)))
```

---

These four exchanges show the same structure: a question about whether a verb exists, a confirmed yes from the inventory, and a runnable demonstration. The inventory is the map; the runnable fence is the proof.

## Carrying the inventory

The value of holding this inventory in your head is that it turns Chapter 1's boundary from a rule into a reflex. When a verb appears, you do not have to reason your way to *is this mine?* — you check whether it is on the tour. Core? SRFI-1, 13, 125, or 27? A Motoi library like `time/*` or `cortex/*`? If yes, it is yours, and it runs without ceremony. If you cannot place it anywhere on the tour, it is a stray from one of the three regions, and the honest move is to say so and find the real verb.

## The inventory as a running test

The inventory is not just a thing to know — it is a thing to run. Any claim about what Motoi has can be settled by feeding an expression to the interpreter. The interpreter is the final authority; the inventory here is a map to get you to the right expression. Every fence in this chapter was a running test of some claim in the preceding prose. That pairing — claim then proof — is the shape of good Motoi self-knowledge.

If you ever doubt a verb, the test is short: one expression, one run. Does `string-split` exist? Ask:

```scheme
;; string-split splits on a delimiter. SRFI-13, yours.
(string-split \"one two three\" \" \")
```

Does `list->string` round-trip correctly? Ask:

```scheme
;; list->string after string->list: identity round-trip for ordinary chars.
(list->string (string->list \"motoi\"))
```

These are not interesting programs; they are questions put to the interpreter. The inventory tells you which questions to ask; the interpreter answers them. Together they leave no room for a confident guess to hide.

## A quick inventory quiz, to close:

- `when` — core, yours.
- `take-while` — SRFI-1, yours.
- `string-trim` — SRFI-13, yours.
- `hash-table-delete!` — SRFI-125, yours.
- `random-real` — SRFI-27, yours.
- `time/now` — Motoi library, yours.
- `import` — not yours, not anywhere in the battery.
- `fold-left` — not yours; R6RS. Reach for `fold`.
- `mapcar` — not yours; Common Lisp. Reach for `map`.

The last three fail because they are not in the battery. Every one of the first seven passes immediately. That distinction — in the battery / not in the battery — is the whole chapter in a phrase.

The next chapter makes that check rigorous. Memory is a fine first filter, but it is not the authority — the *reference* is. When the tour in your head and the reference on disk disagree, the reference wins, every time. Let's go learn to check it.")
